---
/* Where the waste sat — for "What an autonomous run actually costs".
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Two panes, one story. The first is the shape: runs do not waste money as a
   uniform tax, they waste it in one stage that goes sideways while everything
   around it behaves — so the run is drawn as stage columns with one spike.
   The second is what the spike was made of: a session loop that was being
   careful inside a vocabulary with no word for wait — orient at full price,
   read the plan, run the gates, take a fresh reading of a clock that has not
   moved, write a handoff saying what the last one said, exit honestly. And
   under it, the fix: a verb, one line long.

   Column heights are schematic — the dearest stage's measured cost, share,
   sessions and checkpoints are in the strip, recomputed per run. */

const STAGES = [
  { units: 2, dear: false },
  { units: 3, dear: false },
  { units: 9, dear: true },
  { units: 2, dear: false },
  { units: 3, dear: false }
];
---

<figure class="fig fig-spike">
  <div
    class="panes"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Two panes. First, a run drawn as stage columns: every stage short except one tall spike — waste concentrates in a single stage while the stages around it behave. Second, what the tall stage was made of: session after session paying full price to orient, read the plan, run the gates, take a fresh reading of a clock that had not moved, and write a handoff saying what the previous handoff said — honest sessions in a vocabulary with no word for wait. Under it, the fix: a session can declare itself blocked until a named future instant, and the run sleeps instead of paying for diligence."
  >
    <div class="pane shape" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="fig-label">one run, by stage — the waste is a spike, not a tax</p>
      {/* No style attribute carries a height — the kit's CSP zeroes them
          silently; the units live in this component's <style> below. */}
      <div class="cols">
        {
          STAGES.map((stage) => (
            <span class={`col${stage.dear ? " is-dear" : ""}`}>
              <span class="fill" />
              <span class="c-name">{stage.dear ? "the dear one" : "stage"}</span>
            </span>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pane loop" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="fig-label">inside the spike — careful, honest, and stuck</p>
      <div class="row">
        <span class="box orient">orient — full price</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">read the plan</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">gates — green</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">read the clock — unchanged</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">handoff — says what the last one said</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">exit</span>
      </div>
      <p class="repeat">⟲ session after session — ending was the honest move, so they kept making it</p>
      <p class="fix">the fix is a verb: blocked until a named instant, with a reason — one line, and a nap</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    Waste does not arrive as a uniform tax on every session; it arrives as one
    stage that eats a quarter or a third of a run. Which is why the stage
    split is where to look, and the run total is where not to — the spike here
    was sessions being careful inside a vocabulary that had no word for the
    thing that was true.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .panes {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  /* The columns: a run's stages on one baseline. The spike wears the accent
     border — it is the current thing, not a failure; every gate in it was
     green, which is the article's point. */
  .cols {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    border-block-end: var(--rule);
  }

  .col {
    --step: 0.55rem;
    flex: 1 1 0;
    max-inline-size: 6rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  /* The still's heights, mirroring STAGES in the frontmatter — stylesheet
     rules rather than style attributes, because the kit's CSP silently
     zeroes an attribute-borne height. A mismatch shows immediately as the
     spike moving stages. */
  .col:nth-child(1) .fill { --u: 2; }
  .col:nth-child(2) .fill { --u: 3; }
  .col:nth-child(3) .fill { --u: 9; }
  .col:nth-child(4) .fill { --u: 2; }
  .col:nth-child(5) .fill { --u: 3; }

  .fill {
    display: block;
    block-size: calc(var(--step) * var(--u));
    background: var(--surface);
    border: var(--rule);
  }

  .col.is-dear .fill {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  .c-name {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .col.is-dear .c-name {
    color: var(--current);
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .box {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding-block: var(--space-2xs);
    padding-inline: var(--space-xs);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .box.orient {
    color: var(--current);
  }

  .arrow {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .repeat {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* The fix is the one green sentence: it is the success state of this whole
     story, and the strip above the article carries what the line is worth. */
  .fix {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--green);
    border-block-start: var(--rule);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
  }
</style>
